# Wavelength-Tunable Microlasers Based on the Encapsulation of Organic Dye in Metal-Organic Frameworks

## abstract
A wavelength-tunable microlaser is realized based on the controlled intramolecular charge transfer (ICT) process in dye-encapsulated metal-organic framework (MOF) material. The confinement effect of the MOFs is beneficial for low-threshold lasing. By effectively controlling the polarity of the MOF pores, the population distribution between the locally excited and ICT states is continuously modulated, thus achieving broadband tunable MOF-based microlasers.

Wavelength-tunable microlasers have attracted extensive attention for their important roles in on-chip optical communication, laser display, and chemical sensing,[1] but are challenging to achieve due to the limited bandwidth of most optical gain materials.[2] In contrast, organic gain materials, with broad bandwidth, provide the possibility of modulating the gain region in a large range.[3] As a typical example, intramolecular charge transfer (ICT) dye molecules exhibit peculiar multiple excited states, including locally excited state (LE state), ICT state, and twisted ICT (TICT) state, which can be utilized to get broad wavelength-tunable region. Unfortunately, the aggregation-caused quenching (ACQ) and energy loss from fast nonradiative TICT state make it difficult to get a facile population inversion.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) with defined pore confinement can minimize the serious ACQ and TICT losses of organic dyes by weakening the intermolecular interactions and intramolecular rotations. In addition, their regularly external morphologies offer the natural high-quality resonant cavities and exhibit great potentials in constructing the low-threshold miniaturized lasers with high performance.[4] Indeed, the strong microcavity lasing has been demonstrated in these dyedoped MOF microcrystals in recent years,[5] but still remains limited wavelength tunability that may be attributed to the lack of effective modulation means. Combining the variable microenvironment of MOF pore and polarity-controlled ICT process might provide the opportunity to realize the wide-wavelengthtunable microlasers.
In this work, we have achieved a type of wavelength-tunable microlaser based on the inclusion of organic ICT dyes in MOFs, where the polarity controlled population distribution in LE and ICT states enables to tune the lasing wavelength over a wide range. In this confined system, the pore confinement effect of MOFs enhances the monomer emission and hence minimizes the ACQ effect. Influenced by strong electronic interaction with MOFs, the ICT dyes display restricted torsion, which significantly diminishes the nonradiative decay of TICT state and facilitates the population transfer from the LE to the ICT state. This is helpful in building an effective laser level system comprising two gain states. The ICT dye $@$ MOF microlasers became controllable through the introduction of guest solvents with different polarities, which was subsequently utilized to build stable and wide-wavelength-tunable microlasers. The results offer a comprehensive understanding for the construction of stable and tunable lasers in confined systems, and provide guidance for the development of miniaturized lasers with higher performance.
Cyanine dyes, a typical type of ICT compounds, exhibit wide spectrum emission for their unique excited states, which are very suitable for the flexible tuning of the gain region (Scheme  1). However, in strong polar organic solvents, the ICT dyes perform a rapid transformation from planar LE to TICT state.[6] which is a “dark state” undergoing a nonradiative decay to the ground state.[7] This is a main gain loss channel that results in a high lasing threshold; therefore, an effective strategy to reduce this loss must be applied by restricting the rotation of the dye molecules to get lasers with lower threshold and higher tunability. Confined system can effectively reduce the above energy loss, helping to materialize a low threshold lasing by the facile population inversion.[8] As a kind of porous host materials, the pore confinement of MOFs can not only minimize the ACQ of organic dyes, but also effectively reduce the nonradiative decay from the TICT state, which is beneficial for enhancing the emission process from the LE and ICT states, and achieving HLCT (hybridized local and charge-transfer) lasers.[9] The ICT dye ${\boldsymbol{\ @}}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{F}$ microlasers would be tunable through the polarity regulated emission processes, which can thus be utilized to build stable and wide-wavelength-tunable microlasers.
In this work, mesoporous bio-MOF- $100^{[10]}$ $(\mathrm{Zn}_{8}(\mathrm{ad})_{4}.$ $\mathrm{(BPDC)}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{2}\cdot4\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ ad $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ adeninate; BPDC $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ biphenyldicarboxylate) with anion microenvironment (Figure S1, Supporting Information) was selected as the host matrix. This structure consists of discrete zinc-adeninate octahedral building units (ZABUs) interconnected with BPDC linkers. Twelve BPDC linkers connect each ZABU to four neighboring ZABUs, and this connectivity is periodically repeated throughout the extended three-dimension frameworks, generating large pores measuring ${\approx}28\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in diameter (Figure S2, Supporting Information). This dimension is very suitable for encapsulating the nanosized cyanine dyes.[5] Especially, the cyanine dyes holding positive charges are preferred for their charge matching with bio-MOF-100. Hemicyanine dye DASPI (4-p-(dimethylamino) styryl)-1-methylpyridinium, about $6.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in width and 14.3 Å in length) was chosen as the guest gain material. The internal pores of bio-MOF-100 contain a large number of $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ cations, which allows the introduction of the cationic $\mathrm{\DeltaDASP^{+}}$ dyes via an ion-exchange process (Figure 1a).[11] Owing to the strong ionic interaction, the guest dyes can be well trapped in the pores of the MOFs (Figure S3, Supporting Information). The restricted ICT dyes display strong red emission with quantum yields of over $32\%$ , under UV excitation (Figure 1b), which is higher than that of DASPI powder (quantum yields $\approx1.5\%$ ), indicating the improved fluorescence properties of MOF-incorporated DASPI dye. The SEM image (Figure 1b, inset) shows that the $\mathrm{DASP^{+}@}$ bio-MOF-100 crystals possess smooth surfaces and regular rhombic dodecahedron structures. The welldefined crystal structure (Figure S4, Supporting Information) can act as the high-quality reflecting mirrors to provide sufficient optical feedback. The significantly enhanced emission intensity of $\mathrm{\DeltaDASP^{+}}$ in bio-MOF-100 compared with the dimethylformamide (DMF) solution and powder phase, as shown in Figure 1c, indicates the occurrence of an efficient monomer emission in the confined system.
The enhanced fluorescence quantum efficiency, regular shape, and high photostability (Figure S5, Supporting Information) qualify the ICT dye ${\boldsymbol{\ @}}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{O}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{F}}$ system a good candidate for high-performance lasing. When the $500\mathrm{nm}$ femtosecond pulse laser $_{(\approx200}$ fs) is focused on the center of a single microcrystal (Figure 2a, Figure S6, Supporting Information), brighter red emission dots appear on the center of each crystal facet compared with the emission from other areas (inset of Figure 2b). This indicates that the facets in the polyhedral microcrystal form a typical whispering gallery mode (WGM) feedback resonant cavity, as revealed by the optical mode simulations using finite-difference time-domain (FDTD) method displayed in Figure S7 (Supporting Information). As shown in Figure 2b, when the pump density exceeds $45\upmu\mathrm{J}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , a strong laser emission emerges as a set of sharp peaks in the photoluminescence (PL) spectra collected from the whole crystal (rhombic length $d=25.1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ ). Figure 2c illustrates the pump density dependence of the fluorescence intensity, indicating an obviously nonlinear amplification behavior. In comparison, the un-incorporated DASPI crystal displayed a nearly linear relationship between the PL intensity and pump fluence, suggesting that the optical gain of the MOF-incorporated $\mathrm{\DeltaDASP^{+}}$ material had an improved effect compared with that of DASPI crystal (Figure S8, Supporting Information).
The lasing in WGM resonators can be modulated by altering the size of the cavity.[12] Figure 2d–i presents the lasing spectra of three typical $\mathrm{DASP^{+}@}$ bio-MOF-100 crystals (the dye concentration is fixed at $0.44~\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , Figure S9, Supporting Information) at the excitation intensity above their lasing thresholds. The polyhedron with different rhombic lengths $\left[d=18\right]$ , 40, and $64~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ were measured to figure  out the relationship between the mode and the size of the MOF microcavities. The increasing number of modes with the enlargement of the crystal size indicates that the optical modulation is indeed based on the confinement of photons within the ICT dye ${\ @\mathrm{MOF}}$ . For the WGM mode in this work, the mode spacing is given by $\Delta\lambda_{\mathrm{m}}=\lambda^{2}/L n_{\mathrm{g}},^{[13]}$ where $n_{\mathrm{g}}$ is the group refractive index as a function of wavelength, and $L$ is the length of cavity. In this rhombic dodecahedron, the relationship between $L$ and $d$ is calculated by $L=4.9\ d$ (detailed information is shown in Figure  S7, Supporting Information). Figure 2j shows the linear fitting relationship between $\lambda^{2}/\Delta\lambda_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $L$ at $\lambda=650~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . This further confirms the WGM lasing in this rhombic dodecahedron crystal. The group refractive index $n_{\mathrm{g}}\approx2.1$ at $650~\mathrm{nm}$ is high enough to induce strong self-cavity optical confinement and optical feedback, which is beneficial for the improvement of cavity quality factors ( $\bigcirc$ . The measured $\boldsymbol{Q}$ factors of the MOF-based cavities are on the order of $10^{3}$ $\mathrm{'}Q=\lambda/\Delta\lambda_{\mathrm{fwhm}}$ , $\Delta\lambda_{\mathrm{fwhm}}$ is the peak width at half-height) as shown in Figure 2k, which is pretty high compared to the general organic resonators.[14]
As a kind of polarity-sensitive material, the DASPI dyes hold distinct ICT processes in solvents with different polarities (Figure 3a).[15] The stabilization of the ICT state is strongly favored in polar solvent, which enables the emission with a typical ICT feature. As the solvent polarity decreases, the ICT energy level starts to increase, which significantly enlarges the energy barrier between LE and ICT states and enhances the population on the low-lying LE state, resulting in a blueshifted emission. Thus, the emission wavelength and gain region of $\mathrm{\DeltaDASP^{+}}$ can be effectively controlled by changing the polarity of the molecular environment.
As illustrated in Figure 3b, the $\mathrm{\DeltaDASP^{+}}$ exhibited a large blueshifted emission as the solvent polarity decreases (from orientational polarizability $\Delta f=0.282$ for DMF to $\Delta f=0.149$ for chloroform). This remarkable wavelength shift, which is commonly consistent with the large dipole moment of the ICT state, indicates that the low-lying excited state, $\mathrm{S}_{1:}$ of $\mathrm{\DeltaDASP^{+}}$ possesses a strong ICT characteristic. The ICT nature of the low energy transition clearly shows that the Stokes shift can immediately respond to the alteration of relative population distribution between the LE state and the ICT state.[15] As illustrated in Figure 3c, this Stokes shift decreases with the reduction of orientation polarity of solvent, along with the enhanced population on the low-lying LE state at the expense of that on ICT state.
In porous MOFs, the polarity of the pore environment can be tuned by encapsulating different solvents for the strong electronic interaction with the inner surface of MOFs,[16] which thus enables to modulate the emission action of $\mathrm{\DeltaDASP^{+}}$ between the LE and ICT states (Figure 4a). The pore of bio-MOF-100 offers higher polarity than most solvents because of the dense arrangement of negative charges in the pore,[17] which would result in a decrease in the pore polarity after adsorbing different kinds of solvents. To effectively tune the pore polarity of bio-MOF-100, we selected five kinds of different guest solvents, which hardly influence the morphology of the MOFs (Figure S9, Supporting Information). When water molecules were encapsulated into the pristine bio-MOF-100 crystal, the wavelength underwent a buleshift of $2\ \mathrm{nm}$ due to a slight decrease in the pore polarity (Figure 4b). The pore polarity showed a decreasing tendency with the decrease of polarity of the adsorbed guest solvents, along with the gradually blueshifted emission wavelength. This polarity dependent emission relationship indicates that the wavelength can be continuously modulated over a broad range, providing us an approach to realizing the continuously tunable lasing in this host–guest system.
In this mechanism, the decrease of guest solvent polarity remarkably increases the ICT energy level for their weak interaction with the CT excited state, which would increase the population on the low-lying LE state at the expense of that on ICT state (Figure 4c), resulting in a blueshifted luminescence. Thus the lasing wavelength would be potentially tuned between the ICT and the LE emission bands, resulting in a broadband tunable microlaser. It is exactly what we have observed from the lasing spectra of a single $\mathrm{DASP^{+}@}$ bio-MOF-100 microcrystal with a cavity length of $200~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ by loading different solvents which were sequentially changed under the pump intensity of $60\upmu\mathrm{J}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (above the lasing threshold, Figure S10, Supporting Information). When the adsorbed solvents were changed sequentially from high-polarity water to low-polarity toluene, the dominant peak wavelength of $\mathrm{DASP^{+}@}$ bio-MOF-100 microlaser undergone a large blueshift of ${>}40$ nm: $650~\mathrm{nm}$ for pristine $\mathrm{DASP^{+}@}$ bio-MOF-100, $648~\mathrm{nm}$ for water, $642~\mathrm{nm}$ for ethanol, $640\ \mathrm{nm}$ for acetone, $626\ \mathrm{nm}$ for DMF, and $608~\mathrm{nm}$ for toluene. More importantly, the lasing wavelength would be continuously modulated by choosing guest solvent with desired polarity. Unlike the effective refractive index induced continuous tunability controlled by voltage,[18] temperature,[19] our wavelength tuning process attributed to the remote chemical stimulated intramolecular charge-transfer behaviors, which would advance the fundamental understanding of the well-controlled intramolecular charge-transfer processes in lasing, and enlighten the development of miniaturized lasers and other nanophotonic applications, especially in chemical sensing.
In conclusion, a kind of broadband tunable microlaser has been achieved in the $\mathrm{DASP^{+}@M O F}$ host–guest system based on the polarity controlled ICT process. The pore confinement effect of MOFs helps to restrict the space torsion of $\mathrm{\DeltaDASP^{+}}$ dyes, which significantly enhances the radiative process of LE and ICT states, while decease the energy loss via nonradiative decay. In addition, the well faceted MOF microcrystals can serve as high quality-factor WGM resonance cavities, leading to a very stable multiple lasing. By effectively controlling the polarity of the MOF pores, we were able to precisely modulate the population distribution on the LE and ICT upper states, and obtained lasing performances with tunable wavelength over a range of ${>}40~\mathrm{nm}$ in a single MOF based microlaser. We believe that such stable and widely tunable lasers would offer an guidance for the development of miniaturized lasers in optical communication, laser display, or chemical sensing.
## Experimental Section
Preparation: Laser dye DASPI was purchased from Aldrich Chemical Co., and used without further purification. The synthesis method of bio-MOF-100 was carried out according to the literature.[10]
Synthesis of $\square A S P^{+}@$ bio-MOFs: Crystals of bio-MOF-100 were immersed in a solution of DASPI in DMF with varying concentrations from $0.34\times10^{-3}$ to $20\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M~}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize4d}$ to yield a series of ${\mathsf{D A S P}}^{+}@$ bio-MOFs composites with different ${\mathsf{D A S P}}^{+}$ content. Subsequently, the resulting crystals were filtered off, washed several times with DMF until no characteristic emission was observed in the filtrate upon excitation, and then dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $4\ h$ Elemental analyses (C, H, N) of well-dried ${\mathsf{D A S P}}^{+}@$ bio-MOF-100 crystals were measured on a CE Instruments EA 1110 analyzer (see Figure S7, Supporting Information) to determine the dye contents.
Wavelength Tuning Experiment: A single ${\mathsf{D A S P}}^{+}@$ bio-MOF-100 crystal was fixed on the glass substrate. Then the guest solvents were loaded and changed sequentially. The lasing spectra were collected accordingly.
Characterization: Bio-MOF-100 were characterized with SEM (FEI, Nova NanoSEM 450). The X-ray powder diffraction patterns were measured by XRD (Philips X’pert PRO). The UV–visible and florescence spectra were measured on PL spectroscopy (Hitachi, F-4500) and SHIMADZU UV-3600 spectrophotometer, respectively. The absolute quantum yields were measured by using the Hamamatsu Absolute Quantum Yield
Spectrometer C11347. PL images and confocal images were taken with an Olympus FluoView-500 inverted microscope. To measure the microarea PL spectra of single microcrystal, the single microcrystal on a glass substrate was excited with a laser $\lambda=500\ \mathrm{nm}$ , ${\approx}200$ fs, $1\ \mathsf{K H z})$ focused to an $8\upmu\mathrm{m}$ diameter spot (Figure S5, Supporting Information). The input power was altered by a density filter. The emission signals were imaged on a CCD camera and analyzed by the spectrometer after removing the excitation beam with a $520~\mathsf{n m}$ long-pass filter.
